# Milestone: v0.10.11-channel-and-isA
# Covers: Lobby is an alias for the global Realm; bindings in Lobby are visible
#         globally and vice versa.
#
# blocked: Lobby is read-only, deliberately and still
# matrix-row: stdlib-lobby
#
# Reason: the Lobby prototype carries `lobbies`, the registry of every live
# realm's globals object, which the runtime maintains on every realm create
# and free.  A script installing slots on Lobby could reshape that registry,
# and it is shared by every session on the VM — so a mistake in one session
# breaks lobby lookup and `wall` for all of them.  The prototype is sealed
# read-only at the end of boot for that reason, and `Lobby.x = v` raises
# TypeError.
#
# Note that this is NOT the legacy behaviour: in legacy urbi the lobby WAS
# the top-level object and writing to it was the normal way to declare a
# global.  The re-founded core gives each realm its own globals object for
# that instead, which is what `Realm.x = v` and a top-level `var x` both
# reach.  So the fixture's claim is not merely unimplemented, it is one the
# design has decided against; activating it means revisiting that decision
# with a threat model in hand, not filling in a gap.
#
# Urbiscript source (activate only if Lobby is ever unsealed):
#   Lobby.x = 123;
#   x
#   --- expected output ---
#   [00000001] 123
